      "Every claim that sits unresolved in accounts receivable is cash your organization cannot use. Yet many billing teams still learn about claim problems weeks after they could have, because status checks happen late, inconsistently, or through the slowest available channel. Faster claim status is mostly a matter of process.",
      "## Use the fastest channel first",
      "There are generally three ways to check claim status: electronic inquiries through your clearinghouse (the 276/277 transaction), payer web portals, and phone calls. Each has a place.",
      "- Electronic inquiries scale well and can be automated for large volumes. Use them as your default for routine checks.",
      "- Portals often show more detail than the 277, such as pended reasons or attached correspondence, but they require logging in to each payer separately.",
      "- Phone calls are slowest per claim but often the only way to get a specific explanation, a reprocessing request, or a call reference number.",
      "A good workflow escalates from electronic, to portal, to phone, only when the earlier channel does not provide an actionable answer.",
      "## Check at the right time",
      "Checking status too early wastes effort, and checking too late wastes time. Build follow-up rules based on each payer's typical adjudication time. For example, a team might check electronically shortly after the clearinghouse confirms acceptance, then again after the payer's normal processing window, then escalate to a call if the claim is still pending without explanation (illustrative cadence). Adjust the cadence by payer as you learn their patterns.",
      "Also confirm acceptance at the clearinghouse level immediately. A surprising share of no-claim-on-file responses come from claims that were rejected before they ever reached the payer.",
      "## Prepare before you call",
      "When a phone call is needed, preparation shortens it. Have the patient's member ID and date of birth, the date of service, billed amount, claim number if known, and your NPI and tax ID ready. Group multiple claims for the same payer into one call when the payer allows it. Know exactly what you need to leave the call with: the status, the reason if pended or denied, what action is required, an expected timeline, and a reference number.",
      "## Push for specifics",
      "Representatives sometimes give vague answers like still processing. Ask follow-up questions: Is the claim waiting on anything from us or the patient? Has a records request been sent, and to which address? Is there an expected adjudication date? Can the claim be sent for reprocessing? Specific questions produce specific answers you can act on.",
      "## Track and learn",
      "Record status outcomes in a structured way rather than in free-text notes. Over time you will see which payers routinely pend claims for the same reasons, which can point to fixes upstream, such as attaching records proactively or correcting a provider enrollment issue.",
      "For high-volume teams, the phone step is often the bottleneck. Greenline Voice places those status calls automatically, navigates phone menus and hold times, and returns structured results with reference numbers.",
      "Faster claim status is not about working harder. It is about checking at the right moment, through the right channel, with the right questions.",
