      "- Coverage dates: effective date and any termination date.",
      "- Plan type and product: HMO, PPO, EPO, POS, Medicare Advantage, Medicaid managed care, and so on.",
      "- Network status: whether your provider and facility are in network for this specific plan.",
      "- Benefits for the service: coverage, copay, coinsurance, and any visit or unit limits.",
      "- Deductible and out-of-pocket maximum: amounts and what has been met so far.",
      "- Authorization or referral requirements for the planned service.",
      "- Coordination of benefits: whether the patient has other coverage and which plan is primary.",
      "- Carve-outs: whether a separate vendor manages the benefit, such as behavioral health or imaging.",
      "## Electronic checks and their limits",
      "The standard electronic eligibility transaction (the X12 270 inquiry and 271 response) is fast and should be your first step. It reliably confirms active coverage and often returns copays and deductibles. However, 271 responses vary by payer in how much service-specific detail they include. Specialty benefits, authorization requirements, visit limits, and carve-out arrangements are frequently missing or returned only in generic categories.",
      "When the 271 does not answer the question you need answered, check the payer portal, and if the portal is also unclear, call the payer. Document the representative's name, the date, and a reference number. That reference can be essential if the claim later denies for a reason the payer told you would not apply.",
      "## When to verify",
      "Verify before the first visit and again before each subsequent visit, or at least at a regular interval for recurring services. Coverage changes more often than people expect: job changes, open enrollment, Medicaid redeterminations, and plan terminations can all happen mid-treatment. Re-verify at the start of every calendar year, when deductibles reset and benefits change.",
